From 22a805a419c0450939ea13a1346c750382edda63 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Macktherealonereally <macdev@mackthessd.org>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 11:03:19 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] exynos2100: overlay: Make waking from AoD restore brightness
 at once

Waking from AoD took up to ~10 s to reach the normal brightness, and the
screen sometimes stayed dim (XDA 4790286 #49/#50/#59).

Why:
- HWC does not report doze support (USES_DOZEMODE is not set), so "AoD"
  is the panel in normal ON mode and the brightness is whatever
  DisplayPowerController sets.
- config_allowAutoBrightnessWhileDozing=true keeps the auto-brightness
  controller and its light sensor armed through doze. In DOZE_SUSPEND the
  AP sleeps and the non-wakeup light sensor delivers nothing. On wake,
  the ring buffer still holds the old (usually dark or in-pocket) sample.
  The controller only brightens once both the 2 s and 10 s
  weighted averages pass the hysteresis threshold, and then only after
  the 2 s brightening debounce. Each step then ramps at the panel's slow
  displayconfig rates (up to 3 s each), which adds up to the ~10 s
  reported.
- config_screenBrightnessDoze=32 was dead config: DisplayDeviceConfig
  prefers config_screenBrightnessDozeFloat, whose AOSP default is 0.0
  (not the -2 "invalid" marker). So with manual brightness the AoD ran at
  the panel minimum.

Fix:
- Disable auto-brightness while dozing. The light sensor is released in
  doze; on wake it is re-registered with the ambient lux invalid, and
  the first sample is used directly (no horizon, no debounce).
- Set config_screenBrightnessDozeFloat (and the integer fallback) to
  11/255 = 10/254 (0.0394, ~38 nits on the o1s brightness map). The
  previous integer, 32/255 (~112 nits), is too bright for a panel held
  in normal mode all night (burn-in, battery). SystemUI
  (DozeScreenBrightness) caps the level at the screen brightness from
  the moment the device went to sleep, and only then. It does not
  track the room during AoD, so a phone put down in a lit room keeps
  this level in the dark. That is why the level itself is kept low.
- Enable config_skipScreenOnBrightnessRamp so the DOZE->ON transition,
  and the first auto-brightness value after it, are applied without a
  ramp.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 .../base/core/res/res/values/config.xml       | 23 ++++++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 20 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/overlay/frameworks/base/core/res/res/values/config.xml b/overlay/frameworks/base/core/res/res/values/config.xml
index 48da821..594e7b7 100644
--- a/overlay/frameworks/base/core/res/res/values/config.xml
+++ b/overlay/frameworks/base/core/res/res/values/config.xml
@@ -110,8 +110,13 @@
     -->
     <bool name="config_powerDecoupleAutoSuspendModeFromDisplay">true</bool>
 
-    <!-- Allow automatic adjusting of the screen brightness while dozing in low power state. -->
-    <bool name="config_allowAutoBrightnessWhileDozing">true</bool>
+    <!-- Allow automatic adjusting of the screen brightness while dozing in low power state.
+         Keep this off: the AP suspends in DOZE_SUSPEND, so the light sensor's samples stop
+         arriving while the auto-brightness controller stays armed. On wake it keeps averaging
+         the stale (usually dark) lux over its 10 s long horizon and takes seconds to raise the
+         brightness. With this off the sensor is re-armed on wake and its first sample is used
+         immediately. -->
+    <bool name="config_allowAutoBrightnessWhileDozing">false</bool>
 
     <!-- Power Management: Specifies whether to decouple the interactive state of the
          device from the display on/off state.
@@ -160,7 +165,19 @@
     <!-- Screen brightness used to dim the screen while dozing in a very low power state.
          May be less than the minimum allowed brightness setting
          that can be set by the user. -->
-    <integer name="config_screenBrightnessDoze">32</integer>
+    <integer name="config_screenBrightnessDoze">11</integer>
+    <!-- The framework reads the float first and only falls back to the integer above when the
+         float is -2 (invalid). The AOSP default float is 0.0, which silently turned the AoD down
+         to the panel minimum. Use the same level as the integer above (11/255 = 10/254, ~38 nits
+         on this panel's map): AoD runs the panel in normal (not LPM) mode all night, so keep it
+         low for burn-in and battery. SystemUI only caps it at the brightness the screen had
+         when it went to sleep; it does not follow the room afterwards. -->
+    <item name="config_screenBrightnessDozeFloat" format="float" type="dimen">0.03937008</item>
+
+    <!-- Skip the brightness ramp when the display leaves AoD/doze for STATE_ON (and for the first
+         auto-brightness adjustment after that). Otherwise the brightness climbs from AoD level at the
+         panel's slow ramp rates. -->
+    <bool name="config_skipScreenOnBrightnessRamp">true</bool>
 
     <!-- Stability requirements in milliseconds for accepting a new brightness level.  This is used
          for debouncing the light sensor.  Different constants are used to debounce the light sensor
-- 
2.55.0

